flee@cs.psu.edu (Felix Lee) writes:

>Keith Steiger:
>> The more the conversation wanders along these lines, the more I realize that
>> we're running into the philosophical questions of how the monsters and items
>> came to be where they are, and why they're so pristine until the moment you
>> arrive, at which point they get tunneled and scorched and otherwise trashed.

>If you take the stance that nethack's universe does have a past, then
>you need more types of stabilizing behavior: more organizing forces to
>counteract the entropic forces.

This would be one of the more difficult things when thinking about a
multi player hack game. Allowing for many players in the same dungeon
makes it necessary to have ordering forces, otherwise the first player
coming through can trash the place and others won't have a chance.

The system that is used in the Mud type of games, i.e. periodic resets,
is one possibility, but I would prefer a more continous system.
For example, after a while there could appear a troop of dwarves
rebuilding walls and doors that have been broken down. There could
be monsters with big sacks picking up old items and sometimes dropping new.
Shopkeepers should definitely restock their shops if they have sold things.
After all, this is their business!

It should be possible to transform areas of the dungeon, if you put
enough work in it. In this way a player could build his own home,
digging where he wants to have a room and making walls as he pleases.
So the conservative forces should not necessarily restore the dungeon
always to its original state, but it will cost energy to keep it 
different.

I get the feeling that this would be a totally different game altogether.

